To group the 10 VM instances that are running in the same region but different zones, you can create an instance group in Google Cloud Platform (GCP) using an instance template. Although the instances have different structures, an instance template allows you to define a common configuration for the group.
Here's a step-by-step approach to create the instance group:
Create an Instance Template:
Define an instance template that specifies the common configuration for the instances. This includes settings such as machine type, boot disk image, network, and any other desired properties.
The instance template provides a blueprint for creating new instances in the group.
Create an Instance Group:
Choose the appropriate instance group type based on your requirements. For example, you can create a Managed Instance Group (MIG) or an Unmanaged Instance Group.
Specify a name for the instance group and select the region where the instances are located.
In the instance group creation process, you'll have an option to specify the instance template you created in the previous step.
Add Instances to the Group:
Identify the existing VM instances that you want to group together.
For Managed Instance Groups: Specify the number of instances and any scaling policies during the creation of the instance group. The MIG will automatically manage the group and ensure the desired number of instances are running.
For Unmanaged Instance Groups: Manually add the instances to the group using the group's configuration. This involves specifying the instance names, zone, and any other necessary details.
Once the instance group is created, the instances will be managed as a group, allowing you to perform operations on the entire group rather than individual instances. This includes scaling, load balancing, and rolling updates, depending on the type of instance group you chose.
It's important to note that although the instances may have different structures, the instance group operates at a higher level and doesn't require uniformity among the instances. The group is primarily used for managing and orchestrating the instances collectively, rather than enforcing specific structural requirements.
By grouping the instances, you can streamline management operations, improve scalability, and enable consistent application deployment and maintenance across the group.

VM instance is a zonal resource as it resides within a specific zone.
A VM instance in Google Cloud Platform (GCP) is indeed a zonal resource as it resides within a specific zone.
When you create a VM instance, you select a specific zone within a region where the instance will be provisioned. The instance is then tied to that particular zone and cannot be directly accessed or migrated to other zones within the same region.
Zonal VM instances are closely associated with the specific infrastructure and resources available in the chosen zone. They benefit from low-latency communication with other resources within the same zone and can take advantage of specialized hardware or configurations available in that zone.
If high availability and fault tolerance are important for your application, it is recommended to distribute your workload across multiple zonal instances or consider using regional managed instance groups that span multiple zones within a region


Subnets in Google Cloud Platform (GCP) are regional resources.
When you create a subnet, you associate it with a specific region rather than a zone. The subnet spans multiple zones within that region, allowing you to deploy resources across different zones while maintaining connectivity within the same subnet.
A subnet is a logical subdivision of an IP address range within a VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) network. It helps organize and manage network traffic within a specific region. Instances and other resources deployed within a subnet can communicate with each other using their internal IP addresses, regardless of the zone they are located in within the region.
By having regional subnets, you can achieve high availability and redundancy for your applications. If a zone within the region becomes unavailable, instances and resources in other zones within the same subnet can continue operating without interruption.
It's important to note that the subnets within a VPC network are regional, but the instances themselves are still zonal resources. The instances are associated with a subnet within a region but are deployed in specific zones within that region.
In summary, subnets are regional resources in GCP, providing a way to logically divide IP address ranges within a region for organizing network traffic and enabling communication between resources deployed in different zones within the same subnet.

In Google Cloud Platform (GCP), there are two modes for creating Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) networks: Custom mode and Default mode.
Default Mode VPC:
When you create a GCP project, a default VPC network is automatically provisioned for you in each region.
The default mode VPC is preconfigured with a subnet in each region and is ready for immediate use.
It is designed to provide a simple and quick way to get started with GCP resources without needing to create a custom VPC.
The default mode VPC has a set of predefined firewall rules that allow certain types of inbound and outbound traffic by default.
The default mode VPC uses auto mode subnets, where subnets are automatically created within each region as needed.
Custom Mode VPC:
In custom mode, you have more control and flexibility over the configuration of your VPC network.
You can create your own VPC network with custom IP address ranges, subnets, and firewall rules.
Custom mode VPCs allow you to define multiple subnets within a region, each with its own IP address range and routing rules.
You can also configure routes, VPNs, and peering connections to connect your VPC network with other networks.
With custom mode VPCs, you have the ability to customize the network architecture to meet your specific requirements.
However, it requires more configuration and management compared to the default mode VPC.
When creating a new GCP project, you have the option to use the default mode VPC or create a custom mode VPC based on your needs. The choice between the two depends on factors such as the complexity of your network requirements, security needs, and level of control you require over the network configuration.
It's worth noting that you can also create additional custom mode VPC networks within a project if needed, allowing you to separate resources and apply different network policies as per your organization's requirements.

In Google Cloud Platform (GCP), VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) networks provide the foundational networking infrastructure for your cloud resources. VPC networks are organized into different network tiers, which determine the level of routing control and functionality available for your network.
Standard Network Tier:
The Standard network tier offers basic routing functionality and is the default tier for newly created VPC networks.
In the Standard network tier, Google manages the routing for your VPC network.
It provides automatic routing between subnets within the VPC network, as well as connectivity to the internet and other Google services.
The Standard network tier is suitable for most general-purpose applications and provides a simple and easy-to-use networking setup.
Premium Network Tier:
The Premium network tier offers enhanced performance and routing capabilities compared to the Standard tier.
It leverages Google's global network infrastructure, which includes Google's private network backbone and points of presence (PoPs) around the world.
The Premium tier provides lower latency and higher throughput for traffic within Google's network.
It is particularly beneficial for applications that require fast, reliable, and low-latency communication between instances across different regions or zones.
The Premium tier is available at an additional cost compared to the Standard tier.
When creating a VPC network in GCP, you have the option to choose between the Standard and Premium network tiers. The choice depends on your application's networking requirements, such as performance, latency, and cost considerations. Most applications can be adequately served by the Standard tier, while applications with stringent latency requirements or requiring global connectivity may benefit from the Premium tier.
It's important to note that the choice of network tier is made at the VPC network level and applies to all subnets within that network. You cannot mix different network tiers within a single VPC network.

